WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.240
Every sensory cell turns a stimulus into a change in ion-channel current.

2
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.800
That is how force, chemicals or heat become an electrical signal.

3
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.240
But the machinery between stimulus and current differs.

4
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.700
Some routes couple the stimulus directly to a pore.

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.300
Others insert enzymes, or add an output channel.

6
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.760
Comparing those routes explains how a cell detects a stimulus and passes the message

7
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:29.760
on.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.120
In a hair cell, bundle movement pulls on tip links and opens transduction channels.

9
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.980
Insect odorant receptors are ligand-gated channels.

10
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.200
In a free nerve ending, TRPV1 senses heat in the same protein that forms the pore.

11
00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:50.763
A G protein-coupled receptor puts enzymes between receptor and pore.

12
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:56.003
In a rod, light activates rhodopsin, transducin and PDE6.

13
00:00:57.003 --> 00:01:01.243
Falling cGMP closes channels and hyperpolarizes the cell.

14
00:01:02.063 --> 00:01:08.263
In a vertebrate olfactory neuron, rising cAMP opens channels and depolarizes the

15
00:01:08.263 --> 00:01:11.403
cell. Cascades can amplify, but gain varies.

16
00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:16.003
Amplification takes time, and amplifiers also amplify noise.

17
00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:18.863
How do we identify the channel?

18
00:01:19.743 --> 00:01:20.923
Speed alone is not proof.

19
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:26.283
Look for the right location, loss of the native current, mutations that change

20
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:28.863
permeation, and sensitivity in a new system.

21
00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:30.923
Kinetics must fit too.

22
00:01:31.823 --> 00:01:37.503
In mouse hair cells, restoring altered TMC1 and chemically modifying it changed

23
00:01:37.503 --> 00:01:40.603
permeation rapidly, strengthening the pore argument.

24
00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.703
Now separate detection from output.

25
00:01:44.723 --> 00:01:49.383
Mouse type II taste cells detect sweet, bitter or umami through G protein-coupled

26
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:55.403
receptors. They release ATP through CALHM1/3 instead of vesicles.

27
00:01:56.383 --> 00:02:02.323
Deleting Calhm3 abolished taste-evoked ATP release while leaving the cells

28
00:02:02.323 --> 00:02:06.203
excitable. The cell could respond, but could not send the message.

29
00:02:07.217 --> 00:02:09.697
Finally, ask which cell carries the signal.

30
00:02:10.577 --> 00:02:13.537
An olfactory neuron sends its own axon to the bulb.

31
00:02:14.517 --> 00:02:18.257
Hair cells and type II taste cells pass the message to a separate afferent.

32
00:02:19.097 --> 00:02:20.177
This is cell architecture.

33
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:24.237
It is a different question from how the stimulus changes the current.
